  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nToday will mainly be dry with an increase in southerly flow\nahead well advertised shortwave disturbance pushing \nsoutheasterly with the cyclonic flow on the eastern periphery of\nthe broad ridging over the central CONUS. A cold front\nassociated with this disturbance is still progged to drop\nthrough the area later tonight with still some uncertainty on\nthe exact timing and strength of the storms as they push into\nthe northwestern portions of the CWA. Still looking like around\n10 PM EDT for the front to arrive into the area but some\nisolated storms could develop just ahead of the front a little\nearlier than that time. A forecasted 125 kt 200 mb jet max will\npush through the region later tonight helping bolster the\ndynamics with the storms that develop across northern IL and WI\nlater today and this could help to keep storms a bit stronger as\nthey push into our area. But, there will be a loss of most of\nthe available surface instability with the arrival being well \nafter sunset. Mid level lapse rates around 7-8 deg/km though may\nhelp to keep convection more elevated. Bulk shear values of \nonly around 25 to 30 kts also does keep storms less organized at\nthat time. So questions on how intense storms will be once they\narrive still exist. The same timing dilemma also brings \nconfidence lower for any storms along the frontal boundary \nTuesday late morning into the afternoon. The better chances do \nseem to be the far southeastern portions of the CWA with better \ndiurnal warming available in those areas. SPC still does have \nparts of our area within a slight risk for storms tonight and \nTuesday. Showers and thunderstorms seem like a good bet across \nour area with this system but again the strength of the storms \nwill be questionable. \n\nIn the wake of the cold front late Tuesday a cooler and drier\nairmass will push into the area with a northwesterly flow aloft\nthrough Friday. This keeps temperatures cooler with highs \nranging in the 70s for Wednesday through Friday with a few lower\n80s sprinkling in on Friday. Upper flow smoke intrusion is \nlikely to return with the northwesterly flow aloft and in the \novernight periods may make it's way closer to the surface so \nwill need to watch for that. Ridging over the central CONUS \nbegins to push eastward into the region so will see a warming \ntrend this weekend with highs in the mid to upper 80s into the \nbeginning of next week when the next chances for precipitation\nlook to increase.",
